Subject: [PATCH] fix(perf): a second image preload was racing the hero for LCP
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

MEASURED ON THE LIVE SITE, NOT INFERRED. The homepage head carried three
<link rel="preload" as="image"> tags after P2 deployed:

  1. /brand/ew-mark-192.png          the header mark, 10.8 KB, above the fold — fine
  2. white-oak-wideplank-01-room     the hero, fetchPriority="high" — correct, this is P2.5 working
  3. concept-mc-differential         a 1600x1074 figure, NO high priority

The third is FigureRotator, which hard-coded `priority` on its first slide.
On the homepage that component renders seven sections down, AFTER the quote
form. So the browser's preload scanner was told to fetch a below-fold figure in
the same first round as the LCP image, and on a slow connection the LCP image
is the one that loses. `priority` is not a hint — it writes a real preload into
the head before the body is parsed.

Hard-coding it was defensible when that rotator was the most important image on
its pages. It stopped being defensible the moment P2.5 gave the hero a preload
of its own, and nothing in home-client.tsx said `priority` — the tag was
invisible from the page that caused it.

`priority` is now an opt-in prop defaulting to false. Neither of the two call
sites passes it: the rotator is below the fold on the homepage and on
/resources. Every other page has exactly one priority image, its own hero
illustration, which is correct and unchanged.

scripts/verify-preload.mjs prevents the recurrence, and was tested against the
original defect before being committed - reverting the one-line fix makes it
fail with the right message. It enforces two rules:

  1. No shared component hard-codes `priority`. It must arrive as a prop, so
     the PAGE decides - the only place the above-the-fold question can actually
     be answered. HeroBackdrop is allowlisted by name with its reason.
  2. No page passes `priority` more than once. One preloaded image per page:
     the LCP element.

Also: ops/DOMINATION-RUNBOOK.md §2 rewritten against measurement. The odd
permissions-policy was a stale pre-P0 deployment and is now correct; the
Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * is real, is on every path including /robots.txt
and /admin, and is configured outside this repository - every header source in
vercel.json was compiled against those paths to confirm none of them matches.
The runbook now says exactly where to look and why it is worth finding.

44 guards pass. tsc unchanged from main.

